# What each of the twelve territories has, per resource.
#
# `spec/planet.md`: for each resource, a territory has room for some number of extractors,
# and a density that each of them yields. One line per territory per resource, taken from
# the table in releases/first-release.md - `3 x 4` is room for three, each yielding four.
#
# This was `add node` until P-149, one line per node, which was the shape when a node was a
# thing rather than a number. A territory has one answer per resource now, so `set resource`
# replaces rather than adds.
#
# Nothing here is hardcoded data: every line below is a command, and the world is what
# running them produces.

# 1: the landing site. Everything works
set resource 1 food 3 4
set resource 1 metal 3 4
set resource 1 energy 3 4

# 2: few dense food extractors
set resource 2 food 2 6
set resource 2 metal 2 4
set resource 2 energy 2 4

# 3: many thin food extractors, same food total
set resource 3 food 6 2
set resource 3 metal 2 4
set resource 3 energy 2 4

# 4: the minimum a territory can be
set resource 4 food 1 2
set resource 4 metal 4 5
set resource 4 energy 4 5

# 5: food density 1
set resource 5 food 3 1
set resource 5 metal 8 8
set resource 5 energy 8 8

# 6: no metal
set resource 6 food 4 4
set resource 6 metal 0 0
set resource 6 energy 4 5

# 7: no energy
set resource 7 food 4 4
set resource 7 metal 4 5
set resource 7 energy 0 0

# 8: population without industry
set resource 8 food 6 6
set resource 8 metal 1 2
set resource 8 energy 1 2

# 9: rich metal, too few hands to work it
set resource 9 food 2 3
set resource 9 metal 6 8
set resource 9 energy 1 2

# 10: an energy depot
set resource 10 food 3 3
set resource 10 metal 1 3
set resource 10 energy 6 8

# 11: the prize
set resource 11 food 5 6
set resource 11 metal 5 6
set resource 11 energy 5 6

# 12: rich extractors, almost no workers
set resource 12 food 2 2
set resource 12 metal 8 8
set resource 12 energy 8 8
